  "slug": "architecture-naturalistic-information-e2c5",
  "summary": "Recent preprints apply 7T fMRI, information decomposition and network dynamics to test state-dependent brain organization across cognitive tasks.",
  "synthesis": "Across these preprints the dominant thread is that distributed brain networks are described through state-dependent, low-dimensional dynamics. A 7T fMRI and encoding-model study reports load-tuned topographic maps across a fronto-parieto-occipital network during visual working memory. An fMRI oddball study finds that auditory and visual target detection share low-dimensional brain-state dimensions, with modality-dependent later trajectories. Information decomposition of fMRI recorded during feature films maps distributed storage, transfer and transformation in the naturalistic human brain. Pulse-DOT PReFx estimates of regional arterial elasticity in cortical networks relate to cognitive control costs in a task-switching paradigm. A signed dynamic connectivity framework links subcortical routing modes to whole-brain network flexibility and behavioral traits. The new element is the joint use of state-dependent measures and low-dimensional network descriptions across perceptual, memory, control and naturalistic paradigms.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
